/-!
# The p-adic Tate module and the Tate module Galois representation

Definition: `EllipticCurve_TateModule` (`e3517e0d-3db6-5cee-a949-fb0cba7e3bee`), by Claude,
2026-09-05.

For a natural number $p$ and an additive commutative group $M$, [`TateModule p
M`](../def/EllipticCurve_TateModule.html#L15) is defined as the additive subgroup of sequences $x :
\mathbb{N} \to M$ satisfying, for every $n$, the two conditions $p^n \cdot x_n = 0$ and $p \cdot
x_{n+1} = x_n$; thus it is the inverse limit $\varprojlim M[p^n]$ realised concretely as sequences
(the condition at $n = 0$ forces $x_0 = 0$, so the zeroth term is always trivial). For $p$ prime it
is given a $\mathbb{Z}_p$-module structure in which $a \in \mathbb{Z}_p$ acts at level $n$ through
the integer `a.appr n` representing $a$ modulo $p^n$; `smul_apply_eq_smul` checks that when $M$ is
already a $\mathbb{Z}_p$-module this agrees with the pointwise action. Further contents: the level
maps `proj p M n : TateModule p M →+ M`, landing in `Submodule.torsionBy ℤ M ((p^n : ℕ) : ℤ)`, with
`proj_eq_zero_iff` identifying the kernel of `proj … n` with $\mathfrak{m}^n \cdot \top$ for
$\mathfrak{m}$ the maximal ideal of $\mathbb{Z}_p$; and `rep p M G : G →* Module.End ℤ_[p]
(TateModule p M)`, the $\mathbb{Z}_p$-linear action induced termwise by any distributive action of a
monoid $G$ on $M$.

A later section takes as an explicit hypothesis the counting assumption that for every $n$ the
subgroup `Submodule.torsionBy ℤ M ((p^n : ℕ) : ℤ)` has exactly $(p^n)^2$ elements, and deduces from
it that multiplication by $p$ sends $M[p^{n+1}]$ onto $M[p^n]$, hence that each `proj … n` is
surjective onto $M[p^n]$, and constructs from this an explicit basis indexed by `Fin 2`; `free`,
`finite` and `finrank_eq_two` follow.

For a Weierstrass curve $W$ over $\mathbb{Q}$, `torsionField W p n` is the subfield of
$\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$ generated by the affine coordinates of the $p^n$-torsion
$\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$-points; it is shown finite-dimensional when that torsion group is finite,
and it witnesses the project's adic-continuity condition
[`GaloisActionIsAdicContinuous`](../def/GaloisRep_Adic.html#L9) for `rep`. Under the same counting
assumption, `tateModuleRepOfBasis` and `tateModuleRep` package the Tate module of the
$\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$-points, with the coordinatewise
$\mathrm{Gal}(\overline{\mathbb{Q}}/\mathbb{Q})$-action, as a [`GaloisRepAdic
ℤ_[p]`](../def/GaloisRep_Adic.html#L16). Independently, [`GaloisRep.padicIntToRing 𝒪 p
hp`](../def/EllipticCurve_TateModule.html#L266) is the ring homomorphism $\mathbb{Z}_p \to
\mathcal{O}$ obtained, for a local ring $\mathcal{O}$ that is adically complete for its maximal
ideal and has $p \in \mathfrak{m}_\mathcal{O}$, by lifting the compatible maps $\mathbb{Z}_p \to
\mathbb{Z}/p^n \to \mathcal{O}/\mathfrak{m}^n$; it is characterised by that compatibility, is a
local homomorphism, and is the identity for $\mathcal{O} = \mathbb{Z}_p$.

**Relation to Mathlib.** Mathlib has no $p$-adic Tate module; the subgroup of $\mathbb{N}$-indexed
compatible torsion sequences, its $\mathbb{Z}_p$-action via `PadicInt.appr`, and the induced
representation `rep` are the project's own, built on Mathlib's `PadicInt`, `Submodule.torsionBy` and
`WeierstrassCurve`. [`GaloisRep.padicIntToRing`](../def/EllipticCurve_TateModule.html#L266) is
assembled from Mathlib's `IsAdicComplete.liftRingHom`. The freeness and rank-two results are stated
relative to the explicit counting assumption on the orders of the torsion subgroups, which is not
deduced from ellipticity here but must be supplied by the consumer.

**Where it is used.** The representation
[`WeierstrassCurve.tateModuleRep`](../def/EllipticCurve_TateModule.html#L851) is the Galois-side
input to modularity lifting for the Frey curve: it supplies the two-dimensional $p$-adic
representation whose residual representation is compared with the mod $p$ representation on $W[p]$,
and whose ramification behaviour is the subject of separate statements.
[`GaloisRep.padicIntToRing`](../def/EllipticCurve_TateModule.html#L266) is used to move its
coefficients from $\mathbb{Z}_p$ to a general complete local coefficient ring.

